course: dictionary
lesson: 2304
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one it means. The word critical is a good example. Look at the words around it first. «The Ada programming language was created in the late 1970s and early 1980s, first for the US military, and is still used for safety-critical systems like planes and trains.» Planes and trains must work safely, so safety-critical means systems where a failure could cause serious harm.
explain: Clues can also tell you the meaning when the word is in a phrase. «She was critical of my drawing» means she found faults in it, because critical of means pointing out what is wrong. In a hospital story, «critical condition» means the person is very ill and in danger. The same word, two different meanings, and the context shows you which one.
explain: Always check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If you read «a critical moment» and think it means a moment of finding fault, does it still make sense? It does not. A critical moment is one where what happens next depends on it, so the meaning must be very important.
fact: Critical has several meanings, so always check the context before you decide.
fact: «Critical» can mean finding fault, very important, very ill, or the exact point where something changes.
fact: «Critical thinking» means questioning claims, checking evidence and judging fairly, instead of believing everything you read online.
fact: «Critical of» means finding fault with someone or something, as in «critical of my drawing».
fact: In science, «critical temperature» and «critical mass» mean the exact point where something changes.
exercise: «She was critical of my drawing.» What does critical mean here? | *Finding fault with it | Very ill and in danger | Very important to the class | Judging it as a fine painting
exercise: «The Ada programming language is still used for safety-critical systems like planes and trains.» What does safety-critical suggest? | Systems that are very cheap to buy | *Systems where a failure could cause serious harm | Systems used only in schools | Systems made only of glass
exercise: In a news story: «The climber is in a critical condition.» What does critical condition mean? | *Very serious, in danger of dying | Ready to take a photo | Nearly finished with the climb | Very quick and cheerful
test: What does «critical thinking» mean? | Finding fault with your friends' drawings | Believing everything you read online | Being very quiet in class | *Checking evidence and judging fairly before you believe something
test: In «a critical moment», what does critical mean? | Very wet | *Very important, because what happens next depends on it | Very old | Very quiet
exam: In science, «critical temperature» means what? | Very ill with a high temperature | A judgement about a film's temperature | *The exact point where something changes | Trying to find fault with the temperature
